[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite.py: Implemented interface module: concrete exception hierarchy (DAGError/CycleError/NodeNotFoundError/DuplicateNodeError/NodeExecutionError) with message-based __str__ (exceptions cannot be frozen dataclasses), NodeState/FailurePolicy enums, frozen data types (NodeDefinition, NodeResult, DAGRunReport), and system-tier Protocol singletons DAG/WaveScheduler/DAGRunner with framework decorators and stub-only signature parity with low specs.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite_impl.py: Implemented concrete DAG (adjacency maps, duplicate/missing-node guards, deferred dependency checks, DFS cycle detection, deterministic priority/lexicographic Kahn topological sort, roots/leaves, weighted critical path), WaveScheduler (synced READY/PENDING states, priority-ordered waves, dependent promotion, FAIL_FAST/CONTINUE_ON_ERROR/SKIP_DOWNSTREAM policies, terminal/blocked completion checks), and DAGRunner (validate-first sequential wave execution, retries reified into NodeResult records, consolidated DAGRunReport with critical path); collaborators resolved on demand via get_singleton and auto-generated __initialize__ untouched.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_pretense.py: Authored the interface state machine for the DAG engine: model state tracks nodes/priorities/retries and observed scheduler states; 21 transitions map 1:1 to DAG/WaveScheduler/DAGRunner methods with concrete require_arg domains, structural postcondition asserts, and inner_transition pivots; added a seed_root seeding transition, synthesized FakeDagEngineLite plus make_dag_engine_lite_fake, TestDagEngineLiteFake covering every transition and seeding transition, and FakeDagEngineLiteFuzzTest.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/dag_engine_lite/pretense/dag_engine_lite_impl_pretense.py: Authored the implementation pseudo-state machine overriding all 21 transitions with call_target pivots and real SUT postcondition asserts (node registration, dependents/dependencies parity, topological ordering, roots/leaves, scheduler state transitions, is_finished, run report totals); added TestDagEngineLiteImplReal scenarios covering graph construction/query, wave scheduling, and runner retry plus failure-policy execution through as_checked proxies, and DagEngineLiteFuzzTest compiled against the aggregate make_sut lifecycle SUT.
[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite_impl.py: [slug:dag_lite.dag.add_edge.directed_edge] [viol:pret_qa.postcondition_divergence] Facet: node dependency view consistency Witness: node_id='n0', add_edge=('n0','n0'), get_node.dependencies=frozenset(), get_dependencies={'n0'}, get_state='READY', expected='PENDING'. The add_edge implementation (lines 53-59) updates only the _upstream/_downstream adjacency maps and never refreshes the NodeDefinition registered for the downstream node by add_node, so after add_edge('n0','n0') get_node (lines 77-81) returns a definition whose dependencies field is empty while get_dependencies (lines 83-87) reports {'n0'}; the two public queries describe the same node property inconsistently, falsifying the NodeDefinition.dependencies contract that the field list every node identifier that must complete before the node runs and the get_dependencies.return_upstream postcondition. The same edge is also invisible to scheduler synchronization: _synchronize (lines 221-243) only promotes PENDING nodes to READY and never re-derives or demotes a node already recorded READY when add_edge later introduces an unsatisfied upstream, so get_state (lines 321-326) reports READY for 'n0' whose newly established upstream 'n0' has not SUCCEEDED; this contradicts the READY semantics required by dag_lite.scheduler.next_wave.ready_nodes (only nodes whose upstream dependencies are SUCCEEDED are READY) and the current execution state get_state must return, which for a blocked dependency must be PENDING. Both discrepancies are unresolved by the current adjacency-only edge handling described in the # CHANGE header.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite_impl.py: Resolved feedback: add_edge now refreshes the downstream node's immutable definition so get_node().dependencies matches get_dependencies() (remove_node symmetrically prunes incident edges from dependents' definitions); WaveScheduler._synchronize now re-derives READY/PENDING for every non-terminal node from live upstream satisfaction, so edges added after registration both promote unblocked nodes and demote already-READY nodes whose upstream has not SUCCEEDED, making get_state return PENDING for add_edge('n0','n0').
[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite_impl.py: [slug:dag_lite.dag.remove_node.purge_scheduler_state] [viol:pret_qa.postcondition_divergence] Facet: scheduler state purge on node removal Witness: node_id='n5', sequence=add_node('n5'), add_node('c'), mark_failed('n5',FAIL_FAST), remove_node('n5'), add_node('n5'), get_state('n5'), observed='FAILED', expected='READY'. DAG.remove_node (lines 76-93) deletes the node from _nodes and purges incident adjacency and dependent definitions, but never clears or notifies the WaveScheduler's tracked state for that identifier, so the contracted purge_scheduler_state postcondition is not satisfied. Because scheduler state is synchronized lazily in WaveScheduler._synchronize (lines 240-267), the stale terminal FAILED state recorded for 'n5' before its removal is still present when the same identifier is registered again, and the readiness re-derivation loop deliberately skips non-PENDING/READY nodes, so get_state reports the old terminal FAILED for a freshly registered dependency-free node instead of deriving the current execution state from the live graph. The # CHANGE header's definition refresh and readiness re-derivation address only edge changes and do not retire per-identifier scheduler bookkeeping across remove_node, so the remove/re-register path still leaves stale scheduler state.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite_impl.py: Resolved scheduler purge feedback: DAG.add_node stamps a per-graph registration epoch for the identifier, and WaveScheduler._synchronize compares its cached epochs per node, dropping tracking for unregistered identifiers and resetting stale terminal state (e.g. FAILED) when an identifier is re-registered after remove_node, so get_state re-derives READY/PENDING from the live graph; previously observed FAILED for re-registered 'n5', now READY.
[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite_impl.py: [slug:dag_lite.scheduler.mark_running.state_transition] [viol:pret_qa.postcondition_divergence] Facet: running state persistence after transition Witness: node_id='n1', sequence=add_node('n1', None, None, 0, 0), mark_running('n1'), get_state('n1'), observed='READY', expected='RUNNING'. WaveScheduler.mark_running (lines 306-307) writes RUNNING into _states without recording or refreshing the per-graph registration epoch in _epochs. On the very next WaveScheduler._synchronize (lines 258-290), the guard at line 272 sees _epochs.get(node_id) unequal to the live registration epoch stamped by DAG.add_node and treats the identifier as newly registered, overwriting the RUNNING state with READY or PENDING; get_state (lines 368-372) then reports a non-RUNNING state after a successful mark_running, violating the contracted mark_running.state_transition postcondition that the node state MUST be updated to RUNNING. mark_succeeded and mark_failed synchronize before mutating and thereby record the epoch, but mark_running does not, so the epoch reset introduced by the # CHANGE header swallows the RUNNING transition it was not aligned with.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/dag_engine_lite/lib/dag_engine_lite_impl.py: Resolved mark_running feedback: WaveScheduler.mark_running now synchronizes tracking before mutating (like mark_succeeded/mark_failed), recording the node's registration epoch so the RUNNING transition is not retired as stale bookkeeping; get_state immediately after mark_running now reports RUNNING. Audited every direct _states write: only _synchronize, the epoch-aligned mutators, and _skip_downstream (invoked post-sync) assign states.
